<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaundinya I</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hun Tian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaundinya is the legendary founder of the ruling line of Funan in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> region. According to Chinese histories, he was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> from abroad, called Hun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a>, who arrived by ship, defeated and married the local queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-ye">Liu Ye</a>, and became king; a later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> inscription tells of a Brahmin named Kaundinya marrying a naga princess, Soma. The story was later used to legitimise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> kingship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaundinya-i.jpg" alt="Kaundinya I" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kiensvay · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Kaundinya_I_and_Queen_Soma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
